          "intent": "Deliver the full editorial body of the article in a focused, readable format that reflects Pixelwave's bold and irreverent voice.",
          "eyebrow": null,
          "headline": "Silicon Is the New Oil",
          "subheadline": null,
          "body": "For decades, software was the glamorous layer. Hardware was the plumbing \u2014 important, sure, but not the thing that made headlines or minted billionaires overnight. That era is over. The race to build the chips that will power artificial intelligence is now the most consequential industrial competition on the planet, and most of the public has no idea it's happening.\n\nNvidia's H100 became the must-have commodity of 2023, with data centers paying eye-watering premiums and waiting months for allocations. But while everyone watched Jensen Huang take victory laps in his leather jacket, a quieter war was already underway. AMD, Intel, Google, Amazon, Microsoft, Apple, and a dozen well-funded startups are all betting that the AI chip market is too large \u2014 and too strategically critical \u2014 to cede to a single supplier.\n\nThe stakes go beyond market share. Whoever controls the silicon controls the economics of inference, training, and ultimately the cost of intelligence itself. Governments have noticed. The CHIPS Act, EU semiconductor initiatives, and China's aggressive domestic push are all symptoms of the same realization: AI supremacy runs through the fab.",
