{
  "id": "ww-00023",
  "version": "0.1",
  "concept_id": "ww-00023",
  "blueprint_id": "ww-00023",
  "color_palette": [
    {
      "name": "Chrome Slick",
      "hex": "#C8D8E8",
      "usage": "Primary background surface across all pages \u2014 a cool, metallic silver-blue that evokes Y2K chrome finishes; used on the sidebar navigation panel, page backgrounds, and card base layers to unify the glossy digital atmosphere."
    },
    {
      "name": "Bubblegum Burst",
      "hex": "#FF6EB4",
      "usage": "Primary CTA buttons, active navigation states, article category badges, and hero accent highlights \u2014 the loudest pop note in the palette, used wherever Pixelwave needs to command immediate attention."
    },
    {
      "name": "Laserdisc Lavender",
      "hex": "#B8A0E8",
      "usage": "Section dividers, hover states on sidebar nav links, feature card borders, and decorative starburst/pill ornaments \u2014 bridges the pink and blue poles of the palette with a dreamy digital shimmer."
    },
    {
      "name": "Baby Screen Blue",
      "hex": "#7EC8F0",
      "usage": "Hyperlinks, pull-quote highlights, article tag chips, and the glossy gradient mid-stop on hero panels \u2014 evokes the luminous blue of early-2000s LCD screens and browser chrome."
    },
    {
      "name": "Void Pixel",
      "hex": "#1A1040",
      "usage": "Primary body text, sidebar navigation text, article headlines at body scale, and the dark anchor strip in the footer \u2014 a deep indigo-black that grounds the candy palette without reading as a flat neutral."
    },
    {
      "name": "Glosspop White",
      "hex": "#F0F4FF",
      "usage": "Content panel surfaces, article reading background, and card fill on top of Chrome Slick \u2014 a barely-there cool white that keeps long-form text legible while staying within the icy Y2K color temperature."
    },
    {
      "name": "Starfield Magenta",
      "hex": "#CC00AA",
      "usage": "Eyebrow labels on hero sections, 'NEW' and 'TRENDING' sticker badges, lens-flare decorative spots, and hover glows on CTA buttons \u2014 a saturated accent used sparingly for maximum electric pop."
    },
    {
      "name": "Foil Silver",
      "hex": "#8FA8C0",
      "usage": "Metadata text (publish dates, author bylines, read-time labels), inactive sidebar nav items, horizontal rule lines, and image overlay tints \u2014 a muted chrome tone that recedes without disappearing."
    }
  ],
  "typography": {
    "heading_font": "Nunito",
    "body_font": "Archivo",
    "accent_font": "Montserrat",
    "heading_treatment": "Nunito at display weights (800\u2013900) for H1 and H2 headlines \u2014 its rounded terminals are quintessentially Y2K bubbly-digital; set in all-caps or title case with very tight tracking (-0.02em to -0.04em) at large sizes (48\u201396px for hero, 32\u201348px for section heads) to create the condensed, glossy-billboard energy of early-2000s tech branding. H3 and H4 drop to 700 weight with slightly looser tracking, maintaining the rounded warmth at smaller scales.",
    "body_treatment": "Archivo at Regular (400) and Medium (500), 16\u201318px, line-height 1.65\u20131.75, for all article body copy, sidebar descriptions, and panel prose \u2014 its condensed grotesque structure gives the publication a punchy, editorial density appropriate for a tech magazine, while its clean geometry complements the rounded display heads without competing.",
    "accent_treatment": "Montserrat in uppercase with wide letter-spacing (0.12\u20130.18em) at 10\u201313px only \u2014 reserved strictly for eyebrow labels ('FEATURES', 'TRENDING NOW'), article category chips, badge stickers, metadata bylines, and caption tags. Never used at display or body scale; its geometric rigidity acts as a sharp structural counterpoint to Nunito's softness, reinforcing the Y2K mix of slick tech and bubblegum pop."
  },
  "pages": [
    {
      "slug": "home",
      "page_level_design_instruction": "The Pixelwave Home page is a Y2K Gloss landing experience built on a persistent left sidebar navigation layout. The page runs three sections in a deliberate dense \u2192 airy \u2192 electric rhythm that prevents any two consecutive sections from feeling structurally identical.\n\nSIDEBAR: A fixed-width left sidebar (240px on desktop, collapsing to a top bar on mobile) sits on a Chrome Slick (#C8D8E8) surface. Navigation labels are set in Montserrat uppercase at 11px with 0.15em tracking in Void Pixel (#1A1040) for inactive items and Laserdisc Lavender (#B8A0E8) for hover states. The Pixelwave wordmark sits at the top of the sidebar in Nunito 900 weight, with \"PIXELWAVE\" in Bubblegum Burst (#FF6EB4) and the tagline \"The Future, Buffering Now.\" in Foil Silver (#8FA8C0) at 10px Montserrat uppercase beneath it. A thin Laserdisc Lavender horizontal rule separates the wordmark from the nav links. Glossy pill ornaments \u2014 small rounded rectangles with a chrome gradient from Glosspop White to Chrome Slick \u2014 sit decoratively between nav sections as structural dividers.\n\nSIGNATURE MOMENT: The hero section (Section 1) delivers the page's defining visual stunt \u2014 the word \"GLITCHING\" from the headline is set at an enormous display scale (120\u2013140px on desktop), in Nunito 900 all-caps, rendered as a chrome gradient type treatment (left-to-right gradient from Baby Screen Blue #7EC8F0 through Glosspop White #F0F4FF to Laserdisc Lavender #B8A0E8) that bleeds off the right edge of the content area, deliberately cropped by the viewport. This is a typographic bleed move \u2014 the oversized word is not contained within any box or panel; it tears across the full content-column width and exits the frame. A Starfield Magenta (#CC00AA) lens-flare dot (a radial gradient burst, roughly 80px diameter, semi-transparent) overlaps the \"G\" in GLITCHING, sitting between the type and the background layer. This move is unmistakably Y2K \u2014 chrome type, lens flare, oversized display scale \u2014 and it is impossible to mistake for modern SaaS. The signature moment lives in Section 1 and sets the entire page's energy.\n\nSECTION SEQUENCE & RHYTHM:\n- Section 1 (Hero): Maximum density and vertical drama. Full-bleed Void Pixel (#1A1040) dark panel \u2014 the only dark surface on the page \u2014 creates maximum contrast against the rest of the page. Asymmetric, type-dominant, with the bleed headline as the signature moment. High energy, low whitespace.\n- Section 2 (Article List): Shifts to a Glosspop White (#F0F4FF) surface. A horizontal masthead-strip eyebrow at the top, then three article cards laid out in a 3-column grid \u2014 but each card is deliberately non-identical: Card 1 is taller with a category badge sticker overlapping its top-left corner; Card 2 is standard height; Card 3 has a \"TRENDING\" starburst ornament at its top-right. Cards use Chrome Slick (#C8D8E8) as fill with Laserdisc Lavender (#B8A0E8) borders. Medium density.\n- Section 3 (CTA / Newsletter): A saturated Bubblegum Burst (#FF6EB4) full-bleed panel \u2014 the loudest color block on the page \u2014 with centered composition (justified by the CTA close convention and the need for pop contrast). A glossy pill button and a starburst ornament make this feel electric and conclusive. Low density, high color impact.\n\nCOLOR SURFACE LOGIC: Void Pixel dark panel (Section 1) \u2192 Glosspop White editorial surface (Section 2) \u2192 Bubblegum Burst saturated block (Section 3). No two adjacent sections share a surface color. The Chrome Slick sidebar anchors the left edge throughout. Baby Screen Blue is used expressively in the hero type gradient and as link/tag chip color in Section 2 \u2014 not only on CTAs. Starfield Magenta appears as the lens-flare ornament in Section 1 and as the \"TRENDING\" badge in Section 2.\n\nTYPOGRAPHY HIERARCHY: Hero H1 at 120\u2013140px Nunito 900 all-caps (bleed treatment). Section 2 headline at 36px Nunito 800. Section 3 headline at 48px Nunito 900 centered. Body copy in Archivo 400 at 16\u201317px, line-height 1.7. All eyebrow labels in Montserrat uppercase 11px, 0.15em tracking. Article card titles in Nunito 700 at 20px. Metadata in Foil Silver (#8FA8C0) Montserrat 10px uppercase.\n\nDENSITY / WHITESPACE: Section 1 is intentionally compressed vertically (tight padding, type fills the panel). Section 2 has generous card gutters (24px) and breathing room above/below the grid. Section 3 is sparse \u2014 large padding, single CTA, centered \u2014 acting as a visual exhale before the footer.\n\nRESPONSIVE BEHAVIOR: On tablet (768\u20131024px), sidebar collapses to a top navigation bar; hero headline scales to 72\u201380px; article cards stack to 2 columns. On mobile (<768px), single-column layout; hero headline at 48\u201356px with bleed still active (word exits right edge); article cards stack to 1 column; sidebar becomes a hamburger drawer. The signature bleed move is preserved at all breakpoints \u2014 the word always exits the right edge of the content column.",
